etm-powersync-plugins-modbus (1.15.2+etm16) trixie; urgency=medium

  * sunspec: a Fronius battery added after the inverter is no longer dropped.

    The duplicate guard compared serial numbers only. A storage model 124
    shares the Common block of its inverter, so it carries the same serial and
    was refused as a duplicate once the inverter child existed. The guard now
    compares the kind of model as well (inverter, meter, storage).

  * sunspec: the Fronius storage publishes its energy totals.

    totalEnergyConsumed / totalEnergyProduced come from DCWH of the StCha /
    StDisCha blocks of model 160. Nothing is published while the blocks are
    invalid, so the totals never step back to zero.

  * sunspec: new storage action "Block battery discharge" (setDischargeBlocked).

    One ordered, read-back plan: StorCtl_Mod and OutWRte for NO_DISCHARGE or
    NORMAL, the revert timer written last. If the first write fails, nothing
    else is written. The enableDischarging and dischargingRate actions are
    unchanged.

  * sunspec: enableDischarging / enableCharging are named after what they do.

    The bits of StorCtl_Mod activate a limit: true means "limit active". The
    labels now read "Discharge limit active" / "Charge limit active". State
    names and ids are unchanged.

  * sunspec: Fronius storage revert timer at first install, and its guard.

    A storage created during the first setup of a Fronius connection received
    revertTime 0 instead of 300. It now gets 300 on every path. A setpoint that
    limits a Fronius storage whose revert time is 0 is refused with a message
    and nothing is written; releasing the battery is always accepted. An
    existing storage stored with 0 keeps it until it is changed.

  * tools: qualifier-borne.sh requires the SSH user and bounds its capture.

    No default user any more. The journal capture stops by itself after a
    duration (-d, default 45m), bounded on the remote side as well.

  * Public repository cleanup: comments, specs and test notes no longer refer
    to test installations or to unpublished notes. Executed code unchanged.

 -- Patrick Schurig <etm.schurig@gmail.com>  Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:45:00 +0100

etm-powersync-plugins-modbus (1.15.2+etm15) trixie; urgency=medium

  * eastron: the SDM72 grid meter counts import and export in net.

    The SDM72D-M counts its energy registers 30073 / 30075 phase by phase: a
    phase exporting PV while the two others draw makes the export register
    climb although the house exports nothing in net. The sdm72 thing now
    publishes, in totalEnergyConsumed / totalEnergyProduced, two indexes
    integrated from the signed total power (register 52). New setting
    netMetering, on by default, also on for already paired things. Indexes are
    seeded on 30073 / 30075 (no step in the energy totals), saved every 60 s
    and at clean shutdown, and never integrated across more than 10 s or a
    disconnection. Raw registers 30073, 30075 and 30397 stay readable in their
    own states. SDM630, SDM120 and the consumer / producer classes unchanged.

 -- Patrick Schurig <etm.schurig@gmail.com>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:01:45 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins-modbus (1.15.2+etm14) trixie; urgency=medium

  * v2c: the charger now declares smartmeterconsumer, so it has a power history.

    Until now the trydan class declared only evcharger + connectable. That kept
    it out of EnergyManagerImpl::watchThing(), which wires a Thing into the
    energy log only if it declares energymeter, smartmeterconsumer,
    smartmeterproducer or energystorage. Energy.GetThingPowerLogs did not reject
    the request -- it simply returned no rows for that thingId, and the app
    listed the charger as "no history" while its instantaneous power was
    perfectly readable. Two chargers side by side, only one with a past.

    smartmeterconsumer requires totalEnergyConsumed, which the charger does not
    provide: there is no lifetime counter anywhere in the Modbus map, and
    ChargeEnergy (0x0BC4) is wiped on every charge stop. So it is BUILT, like
    sessionEnergy, from the same increments -- and now through the same single
    rule, incrementEnergie(), shared by both counters so that a correction can
    never apply to only one of them. Unlike sessionEnergy it never resets and
    has no session condition, so a charge started by hand on the charger,
    outside HEMS control, is counted too.

    This had been held back on "must be monotonic for life". That was too
    strict, and checked: the engine keeps its own counter, syncs onto the first
    value without adding it to its diff, and explicitly re-syncs when a Thing's
    counter goes backwards. A loss on the plugin side can neither inject
    negative consumption downstream nor erase the log already written. The real
    requirement is "never rewind during a run", which the cached state plus the
    baseline re-read from chargeEnergy at setup delivers.

    NOTE: on an already-paired Thing, things.conf overrides the logged:true the
    interface carries for currentPower/totalEnergyConsumed. The engine's energy
    log does not depend on that setting -- the curve works without touching it.

  * v2c: the lower current bound now reads RegMinIntensity, like the upper one.

    executeAction clamped the top end with master->maxIntensity() but the bottom
    end with a literal 6.0, so a charger declaring an 8 A minimum would have been
    sent a 7 A setpoint written as-is. Both ends now go through
    courantMinimalEffectif() / courantMaximalEffectif(), which take the stricter
    of the charger's declared bound and the IEC 61851 floor -- a misread 0 must
    not open the way to a 3 A setpoint. The same pair feeds setStateMinMaxValues,
    so the app slider and the action agree; letting them diverge would offer a
    value the action turns into a pause.

 -- Patrick Schurig <etm.schurig@gmail.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 10:46:04 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins-modbus (1.15.2+etm13) trixie; urgency=medium

  * v2c: sessionEnergy is now BUILT by the plugin, not wired to a register.

    The Trydan publishes no session energy. Its ChargeEnergy counter (0x0BC4)
    is accurate -- integrating it matches currentPower to better than one
    percent, twice over -- but it is not conserved: the firmware zeroes it on
    every CHARGE STOP, cable still plugged in. Measured cycle: stop at t+1 s,
    zeroed at t+8 s, unplug only at t+127 s. The trigger is the stop, not the
    unplug.

    That makes it our problem, not the firmware's: under surplus control it is
    our own arbitration that cuts when the sun is briefly hidden. The HEMS
    would erase its own measurement several times a day, and an overnight
    charge read back at 07:00 would be 0 -- "nothing delivered", which LM-1009
    condition C1 forbids displaying.

    The plugin accumulates the increments before each reset. In the plugin and
    not in the engine: the plugin polls at 30 s, the engine at one minute, so an
    increment erased between two engine reads would be invisible to it. Reset
    happens when a session OPENS, never when it closes, so the total stays
    readable in the morning once the car has gone. Unplug is confirmed over 3
    SUCCESSFUL polls; failed polls are not counted, so a WiFi outage freezes the
    absence counter instead of advancing it. State survives a nymead restart via
    two cached states plus a transition-only flag in pluginStorage().

    chargeEnergy stays published alongside under its exact name -- the raw
    charger counter next to the built quantity. Only its displayName changes.

  * v2c: registers 0x0BD9-0x0BDB carry AMPERES, not watts.

    V2C's sheet labels them "Power_L1/L2/L3". Measured: a 6 A setpoint reads
    5.9-6.0, a 10 A setpoint reads 9.x-10. At 230 V a phase power would be
    ~1380 W, not 5.9. Published as Watt, the app showed "5.9 W" where the
    charger says "5.9 A". States renamed currentL1/L2/L3, unit Ampere; the
    UUIDs do NOT change, as the identifier always carried amperes -- it was the
    name that lied. Side benefit: per-phase imbalance, which no other charger in
    the catalogue provides, useful for the per-phase overload ceiling
    (LM-1006-1).

 -- Patrick Schurig <etm.schurig@gmail.com>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 14:30:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins-modbus (1.15.2+etm12) trixie; urgency=medium

  * v2c: the Trydan now declares phaseCount, and it is ENTERED at pairing.

    There is no phase register anywhere in the V2C Modbus map (0x0BC2-0x0BD5,
    0x177A-0x1783): the charger cannot tell whether it is wired single or three
    phase. The thing class did not declare phaseCount either, and the energy
    engine's fallback for a missing state is SILENT -- EvCharger::phaseCount()
    returns 1. On a three-phase Trydan that publishes a 1380 W floor instead of
    4140 (6 A x 230 V x 1 instead of x 3) and converts an allocated envelope
    into a current three times too high. The charger starts, at the wrong
    current, with nothing in any log to say so.

    That is the same defect family the energy plugin closed in 3g-2 -- a floor
    that is announced but is not the one governing the start -- reopened on the
    plugin side. Found the evening before the charger was due on the wall, by
    reading the class declaration rather than by running it.

    The value therefore comes from a SETTING (1 or 3, default 1), mirrored into
    the state the engine reads, and re-applied on settingChanged so a rewired
    charger can be corrected without recreating the thing. Default 1 matches the
    engine's own fallback, so an installation that never sets it behaves exactly
    as it did before this change.

    The state carries minValue 1 / maxValue 3: the nymea evcharger interface
    defines phaseCount with those bounds, and the plugin validator rejects the
    class without them. Conforming means the engine reads it through the
    interface, not through a plugin-specific convention.

  * v2c: still no sessionEnergy, deliberately. ChargeEnergy (0x0BC4) stays a
    diagnostic state -- see evcc-io/evcc#28047. To be
    re-examined with the charger and a reference meter in hand.

 -- Patrick Schurig <etm.schurig@gmail.com>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:20:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins-modbus (1.15.2+etm11) trixie; urgency=medium

  * sunspec: stop warning about a missing battery channel on a machine that has
    simply not been read yet. libnymea-sunspec creates the repeating blocks of a
    model from initFinished, which fires during the FIRST block read, just
    before blockUpdated. On that first signal the blocks exist but carry no
    data: every IDStr is empty, nothing classifies, and hasBatteryBlocks()
    answered false about an inverter whose StCha/StDisCha blocks were there all
    along. Observed in testing: the warning fired once at startup
    on a storage whose currentPower then tracked the battery correctly through
    charge, discharge and rest.
    What that false positive cost is not noise. The README tells operations to
    grep that exact line first to tell a broken storage from an unmeasurable
    one; a line that appears at every healthy start tells them nothing. The two
    questions are now asked separately - blocksAreIdentified() before
    hasBatteryBlocks() - and the warning waits for the next refresh instead.

