etm-powersync-plugins-modbus (1.15.2+etm10) trixie; urgency=medium

  * sunspec: make the SunSpec storage visible to the energy engine. The
    sunspecStorage thing class declared "battery" but not "energystorage", so
    the three consumers that filter by that interface never found it: nymea's
    power balance (currentPowerStorage, the figure the customer reads on their
    dashboard), the storage reserve of the EV surplus charging, and the reserve
    of the whole waterfall. Observed on a test installation: GetPowerBalance
    answering currentPowerStorage 0 while the battery reported 50 % and
    "Holding". It was not at zero, it was invisible.
  * sunspec: publish currentPower and capacity on the storage, wired to
    registers rather than declared empty. Model 124 carries no power at all -
    WChaMax is a maximum, StorAval is in amp hours - so the instantaneous power
    is read from model 160, whose StCha and StDisCha blocks each carry an
    unsigned DCW with the inactive one at zero. currentPower is therefore
    DCW[StCha] - DCW[StDisCha], positive when charging, per the convention of
    libnymea/interfaces/energystorage.json. capacity comes from WHRtg of the
    nameplate model 120, and is left alone rather than zeroed when the
    nameplate does not implement it.
  * sunspec: the sign is measured, not deduced. Read only on a test
    installation with the battery power driven externally: a +2000 W charge
    order gives StCha 2000 W, StDisCha 0 W and ChaSt Charging; a -1500 W
    discharge order gives StCha 0 W, StDisCha 1500 W and ChaSt Discharging.
    The same run showed DCW_SF moving from -2 to -1 between idle and load,
    which is why the scale factor is never cached across a poll.
  * sunspec: warn once per thing when a storage has no power source - no model
    160 on its connection, or a model 160 with no StCha/StDisCha block. Such a
    thing now claims energystorage while reporting a flat zero, and a silent
    zero is what made the original defect expensive. See DIVERGENCES.md D-9.

 -- Patrick Schurig <patrick@etm-schurig.eu>  Wed, 26 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins-modbus (1.15.2+etm9) trixie; urgency=medium

  * sunspec: read Fronius float32 points big endian, the way the hardware
    emits them. byteOrder() returned ByteOrderLittleEndian on the grounds that
    "the SunSpec default is assumed"; that conflated the default of the
    specification with libnymea-sunspec's own, which is the opposite. Every
    float32 of the Fronius personality was therefore read with its two
    registers swapped. Measured on a Symo, read only: the model 213 meter on
    unit 200 serves Hz = 4248 0000, which is 50.0000 big endian and 2.3777e-41
    swapped. Reproduced in testing: a meter importing 2.7 kW reported as
    exporting 107 MW, with the arbiter switching every load on, and the thing
    still reporting itself connected with no Modbus error. The meter sign fix
    only becomes observable once this is right. solarEdge already forces big
    endian and the generic vendor keeps its user parameter; neither changes.
  * sunspec: add tools/read-float-order.py, a read-only probe that decodes Hz
    and PhVphA both ways so the word order can be settled by measurement
    rather than assumption.

